    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the build is green (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\n**Contract (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)** \u2014 Added `addressLine1` (optional, normalized) and `addressLine2` (optional) to the `OwnerFields` schema, and documented `address` as the composed/normalized output. Removed `address` from the `required` list so a structured-only request (or the older minimal flat-address request) is still accepted \u2014 the \"either form\" rule is enforced in code, keeping the contract backward-compatible.\n\n**Entity (`model/Owner.java`)** \u2014 Added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` columns (nullable) with getters/setters. `address` keeps its `@NotEmpty` because it's always populated (composed from the structured lines, or the flat value).\n\n**DB schema** \u2014 Added `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 used by the tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres). Existing `data.sql` inserts use explicit column lists, so they're unaffected.\n\n**Address logic (`function/owner/AddressFields.java`, new)** \u2014 Single shared helper that normalizes in place, **preferring structured over flat**: when `addressLine1` is non-blank it normalizes both lines, stores them back, and composes `address` = normalized line1 (`+ \" \" + ` normalized line2 when line2 is present); otherwise it normalizes the flat `address` and clears the structured lines. Returns whether an address was supplied in either form.\n\n**`ValidateOwnerFields` (POST path)** \u2014 `normalizeAddress` now delegates to `AddressFields`; a request with neither a non-blank `addressLine1` nor a non-blank flat `address` is a 400 (`\"address\"` error). `city` remains required as before.\n\n**`ApplyOwner` (PUT path)** \u2014 Now runs the same normalization/composition so the update path stays consistent with create.\n\n`BuildOwner`/MapStruct pick the new fields up by name automatically, so the stored owner carries the normalized `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` and composed `address`, and the GET response returns them. The household-hash, postcode-validation and locality derivations read `postcode`/`city` (shared fields present in both forms) and the composed `owner.getAddress()`, so they operate on the structured values when supplied and fall back to the flat address otherwise.\n\nThe `coreNormalizesAndComposesStructuredAddress` test confirms `addressLine1` \u2192 `\"10 KING STREET\"` and composed `address` \u2192 `\"10 KING STREET\"`, and `functionalityStillAcceptsFlatAddress` confirms the flat form still returns 2xx.",
